===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|+}}

# The state of being [[accountable]]; liability to be called on to render an account or give an explanation; liability to be held responsible or answerable for something.
#* {{quote-book|en|passage=The United States stands at this time at the pinnacle of world power. It is a solemn moment for the American Democracy. For with primacy in power is also joined an awe-inspiring '''accountability''' to the future.|title=w:Sinews of Peace|author=Winston Churchill|year=1946}}
# An open determination of one's responsibility for something and imposition of consequences.
#* {{quote-av|en|date=Apr 10 2016|actor=w:John Oliver|title=w:Last Week Tonight with John Oliver|season=3|number=8|episode=Credit Reports|network=HBO|text=Just think about that: 1 in 4 had an error, and 1 in 20 was seriously wrong. And that is not good. If every 20th Frosty that Wendy’s sold turned out to be a cup of warm goat semen, we would want some '''accountability''' and we’d want it fast! At least freeze it!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2000| title=Advocacy Skills for Health and Social Care Professionals |author=Neil Bateman | page=53| passage=Levying a charge will not, by itself, guarantee '''accountability''': witness the millions of dissatisfied customers of the private sector travel agents who book the wrong holiday, solicitors who over-charge, builders who do shoddy work, garages that misrepair cars, estate agents who mislead, shops that sell defective goods and refuse refunds, etc.}}
# An [[acceptance]] of [[good faith]] of one's [[responsibility]] for something and of its [[consequence]]s.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2021 March 10|author=Nigel Harris|title=It's time to get on with it!|journal=RAIL|issue=926|page=3|text=As the biggest rail player, Network Rail was usually held accountable for failings, but had no authority to change anything to solve the problems. The DfT had been given that authority in 2004 - but consistently ducked '''accountability'''.}}
#* {{quote-web|1=en|date=October 18, 2023|author=Charles Hugh Smith|work=Our Mutually Reinforcing Crises: Not All Polycrises Are Equal|url=https://charleshughsmith.blogspot.com/2023/10/our-mutually-reinforcing-crises-not-all.html|text=The erosion of '''accountability''' doesn't seem to matter now, but it will matter when things start unraveling.}}
# {{lb|en|military}} The [[obligation]] imposed by [[law]] or [[regulation]] on an officer or other person for keeping accurate record of [[property]], [[documents]], or [[funds]]. The person having this [[obligation]] may or may not have actual [[possession]] of the [[property]], [[documents]], or [[funds]]. Accountability is concerned primarily with [[records]], while [[responsibility]] is concerned primarily with [[custody]], [[care]], and [[safekeeping]].

